At this point, don't treat the cache, queue, and traversal as registration actions in their own right. They only narrow the reading scope to the renderer's local dependencies. On the next pass, follow `runTraversal` to examine the tile traversal entry point, then return to this file to look for declarations or branches visible alongside PLUGIN_REGISTERED; the `getUrlExtension` edge is only noted for now and hasn't been explored.

# Guide to Tracing Back _worldToTiles Events

Initially, I treated `_worldToTiles` as the event exit point for a single node on the Babylon side, and started by following `TransformNode` through the code. That was only a tentative sketch at the time: the file header also imports the renderer base class, loading utilities, matrices, vectors, and the frustum, and the exit point isn't necessarily the same as any of those objects.

The entry point is at `src/babylonjs/renderer/tiles/TilesRenderer.js`. First look at the write locations near `_worldToTiles`, then the read locations; if coordinate transforms appear in the adjacent code, note the order in which `Matrix` and `Vector3` are involved. Don't guess whether it's a world matrix or a tile matrix based only on the variable name.

The file header copied down at the time:

```js
import { TilesRendererBase, LoaderUtils } from '3d-tiles-renderer/core';
import { TransformNode } from '@babylonjs/core/Meshes/transformNode';
import { Matrix, Vector3 } from '@babylonjs/core/Maths/math.vector';
import { Frustum } from '@babylonjs/core/Maths/math.frustum';
```

Keep `Frustum` as a search term for the visibility branch for now. It wasn't recorded at the time whether the exit point fires before or after visible tiles are updated.

